{
  "Sit upright and plant both feet flat on the floor. Roll your shoulders back, lift the crown of your head toward the ceiling, and let your hands rest open on your thighs. Take one slow breath in through your nose, and sigh it out through your mouth. You are awake. You are here. This day is beginning.": "morning-001_step0",
  "Breathe in deeply for a count of four, filling your belly first, then your chest. Hold for two. Now exhale fully for a count of six, emptying completely. Repeat this three times. With each exhale, feel the last traces of sleep releasing from your body.": "morning-001_step1",
  "Bring your attention to your body. Roll your neck gently side to side. Squeeze your hands into fists, then spread your fingers wide. Press your feet into the floor. Wake up every part of you — you are not just getting through today, you are showing up for it.": "morning-001_step2",
  "Think of one thing you are genuinely looking forward to today — a conversation, a task, a moment of quiet, or simply a good cup of coffee. Let that small spark of anticipation land in your chest. That feeling is your fuel.": "morning-001_step3",
  "Take one final deep breath in, and as you exhale, set a single word as your intention for the morning — something like focus, presence, or calm. Carry that word with you as you open your eyes. You are ready.": "morning-001_step5",
  "Find a comfortable seat and close your eyes. Take three natural breaths, not forcing anything — just arriving. Feel the weight of your body supported beneath you. There is nothing to do in this moment except be present. Let the day wait just a little longer.": "morning-002_step0",
  "Bring to mind something small you are grateful for right now. Not an achievement or a milestone — something immediate. The warmth of the room, the fact that you woke up, the quiet before the noise begins. Let that appreciation settle into your chest. Breathe it in gently.": "morning-002_step1",
  "Now picture your day unfolding ahead of you like a path. See the morning, the afternoon, the evening. Where will you be? Who will you be with? Let a soft, honest vision of the day take shape — not perfect, just real and purposeful.": "morning-002_step3",
  "Within that picture, identify one moment today that matters most to you. It might be a meeting, a creative block you want to push through, a difficult conversation, or simply showing up with more patience. See yourself moving through that moment with clarity and ease.": "morning-002_step4",
  "Now ask yourself quietly: who do I want to be today? Not what you want to accomplish — but who. Patient, bold, curious, generous, focused? Let one quality rise to the surface. That is your identity for today. Breathe it in like it is already true.": "morning-002_step5",
  "Take a slow, full breath in, and as you exhale, let your hands open wide on your lap — a small physical gesture of readiness. You have set your gratitude, your vision, and your intention. Open your eyes when you are ready, and begin.": "morning-002_step7",
  "Sit tall, close your eyes, and bring your awareness inward. Notice the texture of this moment — the sounds around you, the temperature of the air, the feeling of the surface beneath you. Your body has been in a state of deep rest. Right now, you are crossing the threshold from sleep into full wakefulness. Take your time. There is nowhere to rush.": "morning-003_step0",
  "Begin box breathing to shift your nervous system into an alert, calm state. Breathe in through your nose for four counts. Hold at the top for four counts. Exhale slowly for four counts. Hold at the bottom for four counts. Repeat this full cycle four times. With each round, feel your mind becoming clearer, sharper, more present — like fog lifting from a morning landscape.": "morning-003_step1",
  "Bring your attention to the top of your head. Slowly scan downward — your forehead, your jaw, your neck and throat. Notice where there is tension, and breathe into those places without forcing anything to change. Move down through your shoulders, your arms to your fingertips. Continue through your chest, your belly, your lower back. Down through your hips, your thighs, your knees, your calves, all the way to the soles of your feet. You have arrived fully in your body.": "morning-003_step2",
  "Bring your attention to the center of your chest — your heart space. Imagine a gentle warmth there, like morning sunlight falling through a window. With each inhale, let that warmth grow slightly. With each exhale, let it radiate outward — into your arms, your face, the space in front of you. This warmth is your capacity: for connection, for care, for doing what matters. Let it be present before the day begins.": "morning-003_step3",
  "Shift your focus to your mind. What quality of thinking do you want to bring today? Clarity, creativity, focus, calm discernment? Picture your mind as a clear, still body of water — undisturbed, reflective, ready. Set an intention for how you will think today. When you feel pulled into distraction or reactivity, you can return to this image.": "morning-003_step5",
  "Now bring attention to your body as an instrument. What does your body need today to perform well — movement, rest, nourishment, breath? Make one small commitment to your physical self. Then shift to your relationships. Think of one person you will interact with today. How do you want to show up for them? What quality of presence do you want to offer?": "morning-003_step6",
  "Hold all three intentions — mind, body, relationships — lightly in your awareness at once. Take a long, slow breath in, feeling the alignment of these three domains. As you exhale, let your hands press gently into your thighs, grounding the intentions into your body. Open your eyes. You have woken up fully. Proceed with intention.": "morning-003_step7",
  "Sit comfortably with your spine tall and your eyes closed. Take three deliberate breaths — slow, deep, and full. With each exhale, let your body settle and your mind clear. You are about to use one of your most powerful tools: your imagination. What you rehearse mentally, your nervous system begins to believe. Let's begin.": "morning-004_step0",
  "Recall a specific moment in your past when you performed at your best. It doesn't need to be dramatic — it could be a conversation that went exactly right, a problem you solved under pressure, a moment when you were completely in the zone. Bring that memory into focus. Where were you? What were you doing? Let the scene become vivid.": "morning-004_step1",
  "Now step back into that memory fully. Feel the physical sensations of that peak state — the posture, the energy in your body, the sharpness in your mind. Notice your breathing, your confidence, the quality of your focus. This state is real. It lives inside you. You are not imagining something foreign — you are remembering something true.": "morning-004_step2",
  "Anchor this state with a physical gesture — press your thumb and forefinger together, or make a quiet fist. As you hold this gesture, intensify the memory: the clarity, the capability, the ease. Take a breath in at the peak of this feeling. This anchor is yours. You can return to this state whenever you need it today.": "morning-004_step4",
  "Now bring to mind the single most important challenge or opportunity you will face today. See the situation clearly — where you are, who else is present, what is being asked of you. Step into that scene carrying your peak state with you. Feel your confidence, your groundedness, your readiness. Watch yourself navigate the moment with skill.": "morning-004_step5",
  "Fast forward to the end of your day. See yourself in the evening, having moved through today with purpose. Feel the quiet satisfaction of a day well lived — not perfect, but aligned. What does that feel like in your body? A sense of completion, of self-respect, of forward motion. Let that feeling be your pull.": "morning-004_step6",
  "Take a deep breath in, pressing your anchor gesture once more. Carry your peak state, your prepared response to today's challenge, and your vision of satisfaction forward with you. Open your eyes. You have already lived this day well once. Now go do it for real.": "morning-004_step7",
  "Sit comfortably and close your eyes. Take two slow breaths to arrive. This session is about more than thinking grateful thoughts — it is about feeling gratitude as a physical state in your body. Gratitude, practiced deeply, shifts your nervous system, sharpens your perception, and primes you to notice more of what is good. Let's move through it together.": "morning-005_step0",
  "Start with your body. Bring your attention to your heart and lungs — the quiet, tireless rhythm of your breath and heartbeat. These systems have been working for you through the night without any effort on your part. Place one hand on your chest. Feel the warmth there. Let a quiet sense of appreciation for your physical life arise — not forced, just noticed.": "morning-005_step1",
  "Think of something small and immediate that you are grateful for today. Not your greatest blessing — something ordinary. A warm drink, the light in the room, the fact that you have a few minutes to sit quietly. Find it, and then breathe into it. Let the feeling expand slightly with each inhale. Notice where that feeling lives in your body — your chest, your stomach, the corners of your face.": "morning-005_step2",
  "Now bring to mind a person who has made a positive difference in your life — recently or long ago. See their face. Think of one specific thing they did or said. Let the warmth of that connection settle in your chest. You don't need to contact them or do anything. Simply feel the reality of being someone who is cared for and who cares for others. That is a form of wealth.": "morning-005_step4",
  "Turn your attention to your growth. Think of one difficult thing you have worked through — a challenge, a mistake you learned from, a habit you changed, a fear you faced. That struggle produced something in you. Acknowledge that. Feel gratitude not just for what is easy, but for what has made you more capable, more resilient, more yourself.": "morning-005_step6",
  "Take one long, slow breath in and let all three layers of gratitude — your body, your people, your growth — be present at once. As you exhale, set a quiet intention to notice at least three good things that happen today, no matter how small. Open your eyes. You are already ahead — you have started the day by choosing to see what is right.": "morning-005_step7",
  "Sit or stand tall. Close your eyes or soften your gaze. Take one slow, full breath in through your nose — fill your lungs completely, pause for just a moment at the top — then release it all through your mouth in one steady exhale. Feel your shoulders drop. Feel your mind clear. That breath just changed your state.": "morning-006_step0",
  "Choose one word for this moment. Not for the whole day — just right now. It might be calm, sharp, open, ready, or present. Let the first honest word that comes to you land. Hold it for two breaths. Let it settle into your body like a quiet instruction.": "morning-006_step1",
  "Open your eyes. Press your feet into the floor. You are here. You are ready. Begin.": "morning-006_step2",
  "Arrive at your workspace with intention. Sit upright, feet flat on the floor, hands resting on the desk or your lap. Take one slow look around your environment, then bring your gaze to a single point in front of you. You are here. You are ready to begin.": "focus-001_step0",
  "Take a full breath reset now. Inhale deeply through your nose for a count of four, filling your lungs completely. Hold for two counts. Then exhale fully through your mouth for a count of six, releasing any residual tension from your body. Repeat this twice more. With each exhale, let your mind become a little quieter.": "focus-001_step1",
  "Identify the single task you will work on. Not your list — just one item. Say it silently to yourself with precision: what is it, and what does done look like? Let that task take shape clearly in your mind. Nothing else needs your attention right now. Everything else can wait.": "focus-001_step2",
  "Zero in. Take one final slow breath and, as you exhale, feel your attention narrow — like a lens coming into sharp focus. Your mind is clear, your direction is set. Open your eyes fully, bring your hands to the keyboard or your tools, and begin.": "focus-001_step3",
  "Sit comfortably and close your eyes. Before you do anything else, take a moment to notice where your mind is right now. Is it still in a conversation, scrolling through something, planning ahead? Simply acknowledge what is present — no judgment. You are about to leave that space and enter your work zone.": "focus-002_step0",
  "Begin the 4-2-6 breath pattern to lower arousal and prepare your nervous system for focused effort. Inhale through your nose for four counts. Hold for two counts. Exhale slowly through your nose for six counts. The extended exhale activates your parasympathetic system, reducing mental noise. Complete four full rounds of this pattern now.": "focus-002_step1",
  "Visualize a clear mental boundary. Picture a threshold — a doorway, a line, a gate — whatever feels natural to you. On one side is everything else: messages, errands, worries, background noise. On the other side is your work zone: calm, clean, and focused. See yourself stepping through that threshold now. Behind you, the noise settles. Ahead, there is space to think.": "focus-002_step2",
  "Name the concrete output you are here to produce. Not a vague intention, but a specific deliverable. A finished draft. A solved problem. A completed call. Hear yourself say it clearly in your mind. This is your anchor. This is what this session is for.": "focus-002_step3",
  "Take one grounding breath — in through the nose, out through the mouth — and open your eyes. You have crossed the threshold. You are in your work zone. Begin with the first action that moves you toward that output.": "focus-002_step4",
  "Find a comfortable, upright position and close your eyes. Before you can enter flow, your mind needs space. Take sixty seconds now to do a mental dump. Let every open loop, every nagging thought, every unfinished item surface and pass by — like clouds moving through an open sky. You are not solving anything right now. You are simply clearing the runway.": "focus-003_step0",
  "Continue to breathe naturally and bring your attention fully to the present moment. Notice the contact of your body with the chair. Notice the temperature of the air. Notice the subtle sounds around you. Let each breath anchor you more completely into right now. The past and future are not here. Only this moment is.": "focus-003_step1",
  "Now bring to mind a memory of peak flow — a time when you were so absorbed in a task that time disappeared. It could be from work, a creative pursuit, a sport, anything. Recall the quality of that state: the effortless concentration, the sense that you and the work were one thing. Feel the texture of that experience in your body. You have been there. You know the way.": "focus-003_step2",
  "Begin box breathing to synchronize your mental state. Inhale for four counts. Hold for four counts. Exhale for four counts. Hold for four counts. This is one box. Complete four full boxes, keeping your attention steady on the count. If your mind wanders, return without frustration. Each return is a rep — it is training, not failing.": "focus-003_step4",
  "Identify the task you are entering. Now find the edge of your curiosity about it. What is genuinely interesting or uncertain within it? What would you like to discover? Flow does not enter through obligation — it enters through engagement. Locate the part of this work that pulls you forward rather than pushes you.": "focus-003_step5",
  "Calibrate your challenge level. Flow lives at the intersection of high skill and high challenge. If this task feels too easy, name one way to raise the bar. If it feels overwhelming, identify the smallest meaningful thread you can pull. Find the edge — the place where you have to stretch just a little. That edge is the doorway.": "focus-003_step6",
  "Take three slow, deliberate breaths. With each exhale, release the need to manage or monitor yourself. Flow does not happen under surveillance — it happens when you let go into the work. Open your eyes, bring your hands to your tools, and start with the first action. Trust that if you engage fully, the state will follow.": "focus-003_step7",
  "Sit upright and close your eyes. Deep concentration is a trained capacity, not a mood. This session is attention training. Begin with counting breath to establish baseline focus. Inhale naturally, and as you exhale, count silently: one. Inhale, exhale: two. Continue counting up to ten, then return to one. If you lose count, simply return to one without reaction. Run three full cycles of ten before moving on.": "focus-004_step0",
  "With your attention now stabilized, bring awareness to the front of your skull — the area just behind your forehead. This is your prefrontal cortex: the seat of planning, decision-making, and sustained attention. Visualize it becoming more active, more available. Picture a clean, bright signal there — steady, strong, undistracted. You are not forcing anything. You are simply inviting that part of your mind online.": "focus-004_step1",
  "Identify the task waiting for you. Now determine its type. Is this creative work — generative, open-ended, requiring divergent thinking? Analytical work — logical, structured, requiring precision? Or detail work — careful, sequential, requiring vigilance? Name the type clearly. Each demands a different mental posture, and knowing which one you need helps you step into it accurately.": "focus-004_step2",
  "Calibrate your mental posture to the task type. For creative work: hold your mind loosely, let associations come, stay curious and unguarded. For analytical work: narrow your focus, look for logical structure, move step by deliberate step. For detail work: slow down, operate with patience, treat each unit as important. Take a moment now to feel into the posture your task requires.": "focus-004_step3",
  "Prepare your distraction return protocol. You will be distracted during this session — that is not failure, it is certainty. Decide now how you will respond when it happens. The moment you notice your attention has drifted, you will say internally: return. No self-criticism. No extended detour. Just: return. Practice it once now — let your mind wander for five seconds, then bring it back with a single silent word. That is the protocol.": "focus-004_step4",
  "Take one final centering breath — deep in through the nose, long and complete out through the mouth. Feel your attention settle like sediment in still water: clear, layered, ready. Open your eyes. Begin your task. Every time you return from distraction, your concentration deepens. This is the work.": "focus-004_step5",
  "Sit quietly and close your eyes. Before you begin, understand what you are committing to. Research on cognitive performance shows that every time you switch tasks, your brain pays a switching cost — a lag in reorientation that can take fifteen to twenty minutes to fully recover from. Multitasking is not efficiency; it is distributed underperformance. This session is about doing one thing completely. Take a breath and let that land.": "focus-005_step0",
  "Choose your single task for this session. Not your top three. One. Say it clearly and specifically in your mind — what it is, what a completed version looks like, and roughly how long you will give it. Feel the simplicity of that commitment. You are not managing a list right now. You are doing one thing.": "focus-005_step1",
  "Build your distraction shield. Visualize a boundary around you — a field, a wall, a circle of quiet — that holds your focus in and keeps interruptions out. Notifications, side thoughts, other tasks: picture them approaching the boundary and simply deflecting. They are not gone — they are waiting. But for the duration of this session, they cannot reach you. See that shield clearly and feel it as solid.": "focus-005_step2",
  "Breathe into single-task mode. Inhale for four counts. Exhale for six. Do this three times. With each exhale, release the pull of everything else on your list. Feel the relief of not needing to hold it all simultaneously. Right now, there is only this one thread. Your whole mind can go there.": "focus-005_step3"
}